[10][3] = 162 + 162 = 324\n\ndp[11][3] = dp[10][2] + dp[10][4] = 162 + 81 = 243\n\ndp[11][4] = dp[10][3] = 162\n\ndp[11] = [162, 243, 324, 243, 162]\n\nn=12:\n\ndp[12][0] = dp[11][1] = 243\n\ndp[12][1] = dp[11][0] + dp[11][2] = 162 + 324 = 486\n\ndp[12][2] = dp[11][1] + dp[11][3] = 243 + 243 = 486\n\ndp[12][3] = dp[11][2] + dp[11][4] = 324 + 162 = 486\n\ndp[12][4] = dp[11][3] = 243\n\ndp[12] = [243, 486, 486, 486, 243]\n\nn=13:\n\ndp[13][0] = dp[12][1] = 486\n\ndp[13][1] = dp[12][0] + dp[12][2] = 243 + 486 = 729\n\ndp[13][2] = dp[12][1] + dp[12][3] = 486 + 486 = 972\n\ndp[13][3] = dp[12][2] + dp[12][4] = 486 + 243 = 729\n\ndp[13][4] = dp[12][3] = 486\n\nSo dp[13] = [486, 729, 972, 729, 486]\n\nNow, summing these up: 486 + 729 + 972 + 729 + 486\n\nLet me compute:\n\nFirst, 486 + 486 = 972\n\nThen, 729 + 729 = 1458\n\nThen, 972 + 1458 = 2430\n\nWait, no. Wait, the sum is 486 + 729 + 972 + 729 + 486.\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 486 (from 0) and 729 (from 1): 486 + 729 = 1215\n\nAdd 972 (from 2): 1215 + 972 = 2187\n\nAdd 729 (from 3): 2187 + 729 = 2916\n\nAdd 486 (from 4): 2916 + 486 = 3402\n\nTherefore, the total number of 13-digit sequences is 3402.\n\nWait a second, let me verify the calculations again because I might have made an arithmetic error.\n\nWait, 486 + 729 = 1215. Correct.\n\n1215 + 972: 1215 + 900 = 2115; 2115 + 72 = 2187. Correct.\n\n2187 + 729: 2187 + 700 = 2887; 2887 + 29 = 2916. Correct.\n\n2916 + 486: 2916 + 400 = 3316; 3316 + 86 = 3402. Correct.\n\nSo the total is 3402.\n\nBut let me check if my dynamic programming steps were correct. For example, when n=2, the counts were [1,2,2,2,1]. For a 2-digit sequence, starting with any digit, but the next digit must differ by 1.\n\nFor example, starting with 0: next can only be 1. So there's 1 sequence: 01\n\nStarting with 1: can go to 0 or 2. So two sequences: 10, 12\n\nStarting with 2: can go to 1 or 3. Two sequences: 21, 23\n\nStarting with 3: can go to 2 or 4. Two sequences: 32, 34\n\n