n   \\[\n   \\widehat{MBN} = \\widehat{MBC},\n   \\]\n   and noting that $\\widehat{MAI} = \\widehat{MBN}$ because of their equality:\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{MAI} = \\widehat{MNCI} \\quad (\\text{as} \\, \\widehat{MCN} \\, \\text{is cyclic}).\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion of Cyclic Quadrilateral $AIMN$:**\n\n   Finally, since the angle $\\widehat{MAI}$ is equal to $\\widehat{MNI}$,\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{MAI} = \\widehat{MNI}.\n   \\]\n   This equality shows that points $A, I, M,$ and $N$ must lie on a circle.\n\n   Thus, we conclude:\n\n   \\(\\boxed{A, I, M, \\, \\text{and} \\, N \\, \\text{lie on a circle.}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given four points in space that are not coplanar, how many planes are there from which all four points are equidistant?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many planes exist such that all four non-coplanar points in space are equidistant from the plane. Hmm, let's start by recalling some basics about planes and distances in three-dimensional geometry.\n\nFirst, the distance from a point to a plane is calculated using the formula: if the plane is defined by ax + by + cz + d = 0, then the distance from a point (x\u2080, y\u2080, z\u2080) to this plane is |ax\u2080 + by\u2080 + cz\u2080 + d| / sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + c\u00b2). So, if all four points are to be equidistant from the plane, their signed distances (ignoring the absolute value) should either all be equal or all be negatives of each other. But since distance is non-negative, the absolute value complicates things. Wait, but the problem says \"equidistant,\" so maybe we can consider planes that are symmetric with respect to the points?\n\nBut since the points are not coplanar, they form a tetrahedron. So perhaps the problem is equivalent to finding the number of planes that are equidistant from all four vertices of a tetrahedron. I remember that in some symmetric tetrahedrons, like re