e can ignore this)\n- Two solutions when a >1 (since the horizontal line y=a will intersect the two outer arms of the \"W\")\n- Three solutions when a=1 (the horizontal line touches the peak at x=2 and intersects the middle segments)\n- Four solutions when 0 <a <1 (the line intersects all four segments)\n- Two solutions when a=0 (the line touches the minima at x=1 and x=3)\n\nBut wait, hold on. Wait, when a=1, the horizontal line y=1 intersects the graph at x=2 (the peak) and where else? Let's see.\n\nLooking at the graph:\n\n- For x \u22641: y=1 -x. Set y=1: 1 -x =1 \u21d2 x=0. So, x=0 is a solution.\n\n- For 1 <x <2: y=x -1. Set y=1: x -1=1 \u21d2x=2. But x=2 is the boundary between this segment and the next.\n\n- For 2 \u2264x <3: y=3 -x. Set y=1: 3 -x=1 \u21d2x=2.\n\n- For x \u22653: y=x -3. Set y=1: x -3=1 \u21d2x=4.\n\nWait, so when a=1, solving ||x -2| -1| =1 gives x=0, x=2, and x=4. That's three solutions. So, three integer solutions. That matches our previous conclusion.\n\nIf a=0, then y=0 intersects the graph at x=1 and x=3. So, two solutions.\n\nIf 0 <a <1, say a=0.5, then the horizontal line intersects four points: one on x \u22641, two in 1 <x <2 and 2 <x <3, and one on x \u22653. For example, for a=0.5:\n\n- x \u22641: 1 -x =0.5 \u21d2x=0.5 (non-integer)\n- 1 <x <2: x -1=0.5 \u21d2x=1.5 (non-integer)\n- 2 <x <3: 3 -x=0.5 \u21d2x=2.5 (non-integer)\n- x \u22653: x -3=0.5 \u21d2x=3.5 (non-integer)\n\nBut since we are only looking for integer solutions, in this case, there are no integer solutions for a=0.5. Wait, so even though the graph intersects at four points, none of them are integers. Hmm, so maybe for non-integer a, even if 0 <a <1, there might not be integer solutions.\n\nBut the problem states that the equation has three integer solutions. So, the value of 'a' must be such that the horizontal line y=a intersects the graph at three integer x-values.\n\nFrom the graph, the only time this happens is when a=1, which gives x=0, 2, 4. All integers. Th