f we use only powers of 2, the subset sums are numbers like 1, 2, 1+2=3, 4, 1+4=5, 2+4=6, 1+2+4=7, 8, etc. Some of these are squares: 1, 4, 16, 64, etc. So if we include 1, 4, 16, etc., their individual elements are squares, which is not allowed? Wait, no, the problem states that the set must consist of positive integers, and there's no restriction on the elements themselves being non-squares. Wait, the problem says the sum of any finite number of distinct elements is not a square. So the elements themselves can be squares, as long as no subset sums to a square. However, if an element is a square, then the subset containing just that element would have a square sum. Therefore, the elements themselves must not be squares. So, actually, all elements in the set must be non-squares, because otherwise, a singleton subset would sum to a square. So first constraint: all elements in the set must be non-squares.\n\nThat's an important point. So the set must contain only non-square integers. So each element is not a perfect square. Then, in addition, any sum of two or more distinct elements must also not be a square.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to finding an infinite set of non-square positive integers, such that any sum of two or more distinct elements is also not a square. Wait, actually, even the sum of one element (but the problem says \"any finite number of distinct elements\", which includes singletons). Wait, hold on. If the set contains a square number, then the sum of that single element is a square. Therefore, the set must not contain any square numbers. So all elements must be non-squares. So first, all elements are non-squares. Second, any sum of two or more distinct non-squares is also not a square.\n\nTherefore, the problem is equivalent to finding an infinite set of non-square positive integers where all subset sums (including singletons) are non-squares. But the singleton sums are the elements themselves, so we already have that the elements are non-squa