lternate colors, the cube must be colored such that each adjacent face (front, back, left, right) relative to the current bottom face has the opposite color.\n\nBut the chessboard alternates colors with each move. Since each move is to an adjacent square, which is the opposite color, the cube must present a face of the opposite color each time it moves. So, each time the cube is rolled, the new face that touches the board must be the opposite color of the previous one.\n\nTherefore, the cube's adjacent faces (relative to the current bottom face) must be colored alternately. That is, the four lateral faces (front, back, left, right) should be colored such that when the cube is rolled in any direction, the new bottom face is the opposite color. Wait, but in reality, the color of the new face depends on how the cube is painted. Let me think more carefully.\n\nSuppose the cube is on a white square with a white face down. When it rolls to a black square, the face that comes into contact must be black. Depending on the direction of the roll, the cube can expose any of its four lateral faces. Therefore, for the cube to be able to roll in any direction (since the path could require moving in any of the four directions), all four lateral faces must be black? Wait, but then if all four lateral faces are black, then when you roll the cube from a white face down to any adjacent square (which is black), the new face is black. Then, when you roll again from the black face to the next square (which is white), the new face must be white. But if all adjacent faces to the black face are white? Wait, but a cube's adjacent faces are each adjacent to four other faces. Let's clarify.\n\nEach face of a cube is adjacent to four other faces. The opposite face is not adjacent. So, if the current bottom face is, say, front (white), then the adjacent faces (left, right, top, bottom?) Wait, no. Let's fix an orientation. Let's say the cube has a top, bottom, front, back, left, and right. The bottom face is in contact with the board. The adjacent faces to the bottom are front, back, left, and right. The top face is opposite the bottom.\n\nSo, if the bottom face is white, then front, back, left, and right are adjacent to it. If the cube is rolled forward, the front face becomes the new bottom. Similarly, rolling left would make the left face the new bottom, etc. So, if the current bottom is white, then to move to a black square, the front, back, left, and right faces must all be black. Then, when you roll in any direction, the new bottom face is black. Then, when you are on a black square, the adjacent faces (which are now the new front, back, left, right relative to the current bottom) must all be white, so that rolling again in any direction would land on a white face. But wait, if the current bottom is black, the adjacent faces (front, back, left, right) must be white. However, when you roll from a black face to a white square, the new bottom face (which was previously front, back, left, or right) must be white. But if the adjacent faces to the black bottom are all white, then rolling in any direction would indeed present a white face. Then, from the white face, the adjacent faces need to be black again.\n\nBut this creates a problem. If the cube has a bottom face white, adjacent faces black, and top face white. Then, when you roll to a black face, the new bottom is black, adjacent faces white, and top face black. Then, rolling again would go back to white. But wait, the top face alternates between white and black as the cube rolls. But the cube is moving on the chessboard, which alternates colors with each move. Therefore, the top face's color is not directly relevant unless the cube is rolled over in a way that the top face becomes the bottom. However, since each move is a roll to an adjacent square, the top face would only become the bottom if the cube rolls over twice in the same direction, but since we are visiting each square exactly once, the path 