nt faces of the same color, making it impossible to roll between them without violating the color-matching condition.\n\nSimilarly, if we have two white and four black faces, the two white faces must be adjacent or opposite. If they are opposite, then the four black faces are adjacent to both white faces. If they are adjacent, then their adjacency creates a problem.\n\nSuppose we have two white faces that are opposite each other (top and bottom). Then the four lateral faces are black. In this case, rolling from a white face (top or bottom) to any lateral direction would land on a black face. Then, from a black face, rolling up or down would land on white, but rolling left, right, front, or back would land on another black face. Since we need to alternate colors, rolling from a black face must land on a white face, so the only valid moves from a black face would be rolling up or down (assuming top and bottom are white). However, on the chessboard, moving up or down corresponds to north/south, but the cube also needs to move east and west. If the cube is on a black square (with a black face down), to move east or west, it would have to roll east or west, which would expose another black face (since the east and west faces are black), which is invalid because the next square is white. Therefore, this coloring doesn't work.\n\nIf the two white faces are adjacent, say front and top. Then, the four remaining faces (back, bottom, left, right) are black. From a white face (front), rolling up, down, left, or right would go to black faces (top is white, but rolling up from front would land on top, which is white. Wait, no. If front is white and top is white, rolling up from front would land on top (white), which would need to be on a black square, but the next square is black. However, the top face is white, so it would be on a black square, violating the condition. Therefore, this is invalid.\n\nAlternatively, if the two white faces are front and back, and the rest are black