n \\( C \\) to the line \\( x - 2y + 2\\sqrt{5} - p = 0 \\) is given by \\( d = \\frac{|x - 2y + 2\\sqrt{5} - p|}{\\sqrt{5}} \\).\n   - Substituting \\( x = \\frac{y^2}{p} + 2p \\) from the trajectory equation into the distance formula, we get \\( d = \\frac{|\\frac{y^2}{p} + p - 2y + 2\\sqrt{5}|}{\\sqrt{5}} \\).\n   - This expression is minimized by finding the vertex of the quadratic function \\( f(y) = \\frac{y^2}{p} - 2y + p + 2\\sqrt{5} \\). The vertex occurs at \\( y = p \\), giving \\( f(p) = 2\\sqrt{5} \\). The minimum distance is \\( \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} = 2 \\).\n\n### Final Answer\n\n1. The equation of the trajectory \\( C \\) is \\(\\boxed{y^2 = p(x - 2p)}\\).\n\n2. The minimum value of \\( d \\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "Given a parabola \\(y^{2} = 2px\\) where \\(p > 0\\), two points \\(A\\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\\right)\\) and \\(B\\left(x_{2}, y_{2}\\right)\\) lie on this parabola, and the origin \\(O\\) is the coordinate origin such that \\(OA \\perp OB\\).\n\n#### Part 1: Finding the Trace Equation of the Midpoint of Segment \\(AB\\)\n1. Since both points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on the parabola \\(y^{2} = 2px\\), we have:\n\n   \\[\n   y_{1}^{2} = 2px_{1} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y_{2}^{2} = 2px_{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. Given that \\(OA \\perp OB\\), the dot product of vectors \\(\\overrightarrow{OA}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OB}\\) is zero:\n\n   \\[\n   x_{1}x_{2} + y_{1}y_{2} = 0\n   \\]\n\n3. Substituting \\(x_{1}\\) and \\(x_{2}\\) from the parabola equation in the dot product condition:\n\n   \\[\n   x_{1}x_{2} = \\frac{y_{1}^{2}y_{2}^{2}}{4p^{2}} = \\frac{(y_{1}y_{2})^{2}}{4p^{2}}\n   \\]\n\n4. Using the perpendicular condition:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(y_{1}y_{2})^{2}}{4p^{2}} + y_{1}y_{2} = 0\n   \\]\n\n5. Let \\(y_{1}y_{2} = k\\). Then we have:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k^{2}}{4p^{2}} + k = 0\n   \\]\n\n6. Solving for \\(k\\):\n\n   \\[\n   k \\left( \\frac{k}{4p^{2}} + 1 \\right) = 0\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   k = -4p^{2}\n   \\]\n\n7. Denote the coordinates of the midpoint \\(M\\) of segment \\(AB\\) as \\((x, y)\\). Thus:\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = \\frac{y_{1} + y_{2}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. Substituting \\(x_{1}\\) and \\(x_{2}\\):\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( x_{1} + x_{2} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{y_{1}^{2}}{2p} + \\frac{y_{2}^{2}}{2p} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{1}{4p} \\left( y_{1}^{2} + y_{2}^{2} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n9. Since:\n\n   \\[\n   y_{1}^{2} + y_{2}^{2} = (y_{1} + y_{2})^{2} - 2y_{1}y_{2}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   y_{1}^{2} + y_{2}^{2} = 4y^{2} - 2(-4p^{2})\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   y_{1}^{2} + y_{2}^{2} = 4y^{2} + 8p^{2}\n   \\]\n\n10. Therefore, the x-coordinate of the midpoint is given by:\n\n    \\[\n    x = \\frac{1}{4p} [4y^{2} + 8p^{2}] = y^{2}/p + 2p\n    \\]\n\n11. Hence, the equation for the trace of the midpoint \\(C\\) is:\n\n    \\[\n    y^{2} = px - 2p^{2}\n    \\]\n\n#### Part 2: Finding the Minimum Distance \\(d\\)\n1. Given the distance from a point \\((x, y)\\) to the line \\(x - 2y + 2\\sqrt{5} - p = 0\\) is:\n\n   \\[\n   d = \\frac{|x - 2y + 2\\sqrt{5} - p|}{\\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}} = \\frac{|x - 2y + 2\\sqrt{5} - p|}{\\sqrt{5}}\n   \\]\n\n2. Substituting \\(x = \\frac{y^2 + 2p^{2}}{p}\\):\n\n   \\[\n   d = \\frac{\\left| \\frac{y^{2} + 2p^{2}}{p} - 2y + 2\\sqrt{5} - p \\right|}{\\sqrt{5}}\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplifying the expression inside the absolute value:\n\n   \\[\n   d = \\frac{\\left| \\frac{y^{2} + 2p^{2} - 2yp + 2p\\sqrt{5} - p^{2}}{p} \\right|}{\\sqrt{5}}\n   \\]\n\n4. Rewrite to see it more clearly:\n\n   \\[\n   d = \\frac{\\left| y^{2} - 2yp + 2\\sqrt{5}p + p^{2} \\right|}{p \\sqrt{5}} = \\frac{|(y - p)^{2} + 2\\sqrt{5}p|}{\\sqrt{5} p}\n   \\]\n\n5. Knowing that \\((y - p)^{2} \\geq 0\\), the minimum value of \\(d\\) is reached when \\((y - p)^{2} = 0\\).\n\n   \\[\n   d_{\\min} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{5} p}{\\sqrt{5} p} = 2\n   \\]\n\n6. When \\(y = p\\), then \\(x = \\frac{p^2 + 2p^2}{p