urface.\n   - The angle of incidence (with respect to the normal) is \\(90^\\circ - \\alpha\\).\n\n2. **Mirror Rotation**:\n   - The mirror is rotated by an angle \\(\\beta\\) around the projection of the incident beam onto the mirror.\n   - This rotation changes the normal vector of the mirror. The new normal vector forms an angle \\(\\beta\\) with the original normal.\n\n3. **Reflection Direction Calculation**:\n   - The original reflected beam direction is calculated using the law of reflection.\n   - After rotation, the new normal vector and the incident beam direction are used to compute the new reflected beam direction using vector reflection formulas.\n\n4. **Angle Between Reflected Beams**:\n   - The angle \\(\\phi\\) between the original and new reflected beams is found using the dot product formula for vectors.\n\n5. **Trigonometric Simplification**:\n   - The derived angle \\(\\phi\\) is simplified using trigonometric identities and verified with specific cases.\n\n**Final Answer**:\nThe angle by which the reflected beam deviates as a result of the mirror's rotation is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2 \\arcsin(\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": ": \nThe problem requires determining the angle by which the reflected ray of light deviates when a mirror is rotated.\n\n1. **Initial Setup:**\n   \n   Let \\( B \\) be the point of incidence where the light ray hits the mirror, and \\( A \\) be a point on the incident ray distinct from \\( B \\). Let \\( K \\) and \\( L \\) be the projections of \\( A \\) onto the mirror in its initial and rotated positions, respectively. Let \\( A_1 \\) and \\( A_2 \\) be points symmetrical to \\( A \\) relative to the mirror's initial and rotated positions respectively.\n\n2. **Incident Ray:**\n\n   Given that the incident angle is \\( \\alpha \\).\n\n3. **Reflections and Symmetry:**\n\n   - The point \\( A_1 \\) is the reflection of \\( A \\) across the original position of the mirror.\n   - The point \\( 